From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 05:53:26 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] NextBSD: loader images, no double colour reduction through
 GOP Blt()

gfx_fb_putimage() reduces each channel to the framebuffer's width
("rough colorspace reduction for 15/16 bit colors") before it blits.
The direct-write path needs that: gfxfb_blt_buffer_to_video() masks
each channel into place. GOP Blt() does not: it takes 8 bits a channel
and packs them for the framebuffer itself, so on a 15/16 bpp GOP every
image was reduced twice. Under U-Boot's 16 bpp GOP on a Raspberry Pi
500+ the boot splash's white spinner (255,255,255) became (31,63,31)
and was packed as red 3/31, green 15/63, blue 3/31, a faint green;
the dark cube was near black.

Reduce only when the blit will not go through GOP Blt(), the same
condition gfxfb_blt() uses. 32 bpp screens shift by 0 either way and
are unchanged.

Part of nextbsd/nextbsd#542 (nextbsd-freebsd-compat#90).

Carried out-of-tree for NextBSD.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 stand/common/gfx_fb.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/stand/common/gfx_fb.c b/stand/common/gfx_fb.c
index 98b4c0190..71a636109 100644
--- a/stand/common/gfx_fb.c
+++ b/stand/common/gfx_fb.c
@@ -849,6 +849,18 @@ gfxfb_shadow_fill(uint32_t *BltBuffer,
 	}
 }
 
+/* NextBSD: will gfxfb_blt() go through GOP Blt()? */
+static bool
+gfx_fb_blt_by_gop(void)
+{
+#if defined(EFI)
+	return (gfx_state.tg_fb_type == FB_GOP && !ignore_gop_blt &&
+	    boot_services_active);
+#else
+	return (false);
+#endif
+}
+
 int
 gfxfb_blt(void *BltBuffer, GFXFB_BLT_OPERATION BltOperation,
     uint32_t SourceX, uint32_t SourceY,
@@ -1995,6 +2007,15 @@ gfx_fb_putimage(png_t *png, uint32_t ux1, uint32_t uy1, uint32_t ux2,
 	    ffs(gfx_state.tg_fb.fb_mask_green) + 1);
 	bs = 8 - (fls(gfx_state.tg_fb.fb_mask_blue) -
 	    ffs(gfx_state.tg_fb.fb_mask_blue) + 1);
+	/*
+	 * NextBSD: GOP Blt() takes 8 bits a channel and packs them for the
+	 * framebuffer itself.  Only the direct-write path wants channels
+	 * already reduced to the framebuffer's widths; reducing for Blt()
+	 * as well darkens a 15/16 bit screen twice (white came out a faint
+	 * green under U-Boot's 16 bpp GOP on a Raspberry Pi 5).
+	 */
+	if (gfx_fb_blt_by_gop())
+		rs = gs = bs = 0;
 
 	uint32_t hc = 0;
 	for (y = 0; y < fheight; y++) {
-- 
